{"skill_id":"convergence-rates","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"convergence-rates-r1-q1","version":1,"round":1,"type":"mc","prompt":"A sequence of errors \\(e_k \\to 0\\) converges linearly when the successive ratios \\(e_{k+1}/e_k\\) tend to a limit \\(\\mu\\) with","options":["\\(\\mu > 1\\)","\\(\\mu = 0\\)","\\(0 < \\mu < 1\\)","\\(\\mu = 1\\)"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Linear convergence means the error shrinks by a factor that settles strictly between 0 and 1. A limit above 1 would make the error grow. A limit of 0 is superlinear, which is faster than linear. A limit of 1, with the error still going to 0, is the sublinear pattern of sequences such as \\(1/k\\).","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a falling curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">k</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">e</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,41.9 52,44.8 55.1,47.5 58.1,50.2 61.1,52.8 64.1,55.3 67.2,57.7 70.2,60.1 73.2,62.4 76.2,64.6 79.2,66.8 82.3,68.9 85.3,71 88.3,73 91.3,74.9 94.4,76.8 97.4,78.6 100.4,80.4 103.4,82.1 106.4,83.8 109.5,85.4 112.5,87 115.5,88.5 118.5,90 121.6,91.4 124.6,92.8 127.6,94.2 130.6,95.5 133.6,96.8 136.7,98.1 139.7,99.3 142.7,100.4 145.7,101.6 148.8,102.7 151.8,103.8 154.8,104.8 157.8,105.8 160.8,106.8 163.9,107.8 166.9,108.7 169.9,109.6 172.9,110.5 176,111.4 179,112.2 182,113 185,113.8 188,114.5 191.1,115.3 194.1,116 197.1,116.7 200.1,117.4 203.2,118 206.2,118.7 209.2,119.3 212.2,119.9 215.2,120.5 218.3,121 221.3,121.6 224.3,122.1 227.3,122.6 230.4,123.1 233.4,123.6 236.4,124.1 239.4,124.6 242.4,125 245.5,125.4 248.5,125.9 251.5,126.3 254.5,126.7 257.6,127.1 260.6,127.4 263.6,127.8 266.6,128.2 269.6,128.5 272.7,128.8 275.7,129.2 278.7,129.5 281.7,129.8 284.8,130.1 287.8,130.4 290.8,130.7 293.8,130.9 296.8,131.2 299.9,131.5 302.9,131.7 305.9,132 308.9,132.2 312,132.4 315,132.6 318,132.9\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A curve that falls toward the horizontal axis."},{"id":"convergence-rates-r1-q2","version":1,"round":1,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Three successive errors are \\(0.9\\), \\(0.45\\), and \\(0.225\\). What is the constant successive ratio \\(e_{k+1}/e_k\\)?","answer":0.5,"unit":"1","tolerance":0.02,"explanation":"Divide \\(0.45\\) by \\(0.9\\), or \\(0.225\\) by \\(0.45\\). Both quotients are \\(1/2\\). The sequence is linear with rate \\(1/2\\). Reporting \\(2\\) inverts the ratio. Reporting \\(0.225\\) repeats the last error. Reporting \\(0.9\\) repeats the first.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a falling curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">k</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">e</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,41.9 52,44.8 55.1,47.5 58.1,50.2 61.1,52.8 64.1,55.3 67.2,57.7 70.2,60.1 73.2,62.4 76.2,64.6 79.2,66.8 82.3,68.9 85.3,71 88.3,73 91.3,74.9 94.4,76.8 97.4,78.6 100.4,80.4 103.4,82.1 106.4,83.8 109.5,85.4 112.5,87 115.5,88.5 118.5,90 121.6,91.4 124.6,92.8 127.6,94.2 130.6,95.5 133.6,96.8 136.7,98.1 139.7,99.3 142.7,100.4 145.7,101.6 148.8,102.7 151.8,103.8 154.8,104.8 157.8,105.8 160.8,106.8 163.9,107.8 166.9,108.7 169.9,109.6 172.9,110.5 176,111.4 179,112.2 182,113 185,113.8 188,114.5 191.1,115.3 194.1,116 197.1,116.7 200.1,117.4 203.2,118 206.2,118.7 209.2,119.3 212.2,119.9 215.2,120.5 218.3,121 221.3,121.6 224.3,122.1 227.3,122.6 230.4,123.1 233.4,123.6 236.4,124.1 239.4,124.6 242.4,125 245.5,125.4 248.5,125.9 251.5,126.3 254.5,126.7 257.6,127.1 260.6,127.4 263.6,127.8 266.6,128.2 269.6,128.5 272.7,128.8 275.7,129.2 278.7,129.5 281.7,129.8 284.8,130.1 287.8,130.4 290.8,130.7 293.8,130.9 296.8,131.2 299.9,131.5 302.9,131.7 305.9,132 308.9,132.2 312,132.4 315,132.6 318,132.9\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A curve that falls toward the horizontal axis."},{"id":"convergence-rates-r1-q3","version":1,"round":1,"type":"mc","prompt":"The errors \\(e_k = 1/k\\) for \\(k \\ge 1\\) go to zero. Their convergence is","options":["quadratic, because the errors are a rational function of \\(k\\)","linear, because they decrease at every step","superlinear, because they have no constant factor","sublinear, because the successive ratios tend to 1"],"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The ratio \\((1/(k+1))/(1/k) = k/(k+1)\\) tends to 1. The errors go to zero, but not by a factor bounded below 1, so the rate is sublinear. Decreasing at every step is not enough for a linear rate. Superlinear convergence requires the ratio to tend to 0. A rational formula is not the definition of a quadratic rate.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a slowly falling curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">k</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">e</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,43.1 52,46.9 55.1,50.5 58.1,53.8 61.1,57 64.1,60 67.2,62.8 70.2,65.5 73.2,68 76.2,70.4 79.2,72.7 82.3,74.9 85.3,77 88.3,79 91.3,80.9 94.4,82.7 97.4,84.4 100.4,86.1 103.4,87.7 106.4,89.2 109.5,90.7 112.5,92.1 115.5,93.5 118.5,94.8 121.6,96 124.6,97.2 127.6,98.4 130.6,99.6 133.6,100.7 136.7,101.7 139.7,102.7 142.7,103.7 145.7,104.7 148.8,105.6 151.8,106.5 154.8,107.4 157.8,108.2 160.8,109.1 163.9,109.8 166.9,110.6 169.9,111.4 172.9,112.1 176,112.8 179,113.5 182,114.2 185,114.8 188,115.5 191.1,116.1 194.1,116.7 197.1,117.3 200.1,117.9 203.2,118.4 206.2,119 209.2,119.5 212.2,120 215.2,120.6 218.3,121.1 221.3,121.5 224.3,122 227.3,122.5 230.4,122.9 233.4,123.4 236.4,123.8 239.4,124.3 242.4,124.7 245.5,125.1 248.5,125.5 251.5,125.9 254.5,126.3 257.6,126.6 260.6,127 263.6,127.4 266.6,127.7 269.6,128.1 272.7,128.4 275.7,128.8 278.7,129.1 281.7,129.4 284.8,129.7 287.8,130 290.8,130.3 293.8,130.6 296.8,130.9 299.9,131.2 302.9,131.5 305.9,131.8 308.9,132.1 312,132.3 315,132.6 318,132.9\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A curve that drops at first and then flattens."},{"id":"convergence-rates-r2-q1","version":1,"round":2,"type":"order","prompt":"Put these checks in the order you would apply them to a positive error sequence that tends to zero and might be linear, superlinear, or quadratic. Each step uses the result of the step before it.","explanation":"A rate is a statement about an error that tends to zero, so that check comes first. The successive ratios are the next measurement, and a limit strictly inside \\((0, 1)\\) already classifies the sequence as linear. Only a ratio limit of 0 opens the superlinear case, and the square in the denominator is the further test that separates quadratic from merely superlinear. Squaring before the successive ratios are known skips the linear case.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a falling curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">k</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">e</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,41.9 52,44.8 55.1,47.5 58.1,50.2 61.1,52.8 64.1,55.3 67.2,57.7 70.2,60.1 73.2,62.4 76.2,64.6 79.2,66.8 82.3,68.9 85.3,71 88.3,73 91.3,74.9 94.4,76.8 97.4,78.6 100.4,80.4 103.4,82.1 106.4,83.8 109.5,85.4 112.5,87 115.5,88.5 118.5,90 121.6,91.4 124.6,92.8 127.6,94.2 130.6,95.5 133.6,96.8 136.7,98.1 139.7,99.3 142.7,100.4 145.7,101.6 148.8,102.7 151.8,103.8 154.8,104.8 157.8,105.8 160.8,106.8 163.9,107.8 166.9,108.7 169.9,109.6 172.9,110.5 176,111.4 179,112.2 182,113 185,113.8 188,114.5 191.1,115.3 194.1,116 197.1,116.7 200.1,117.4 203.2,118 206.2,118.7 209.2,119.3 212.2,119.9 215.2,120.5 218.3,121 221.3,121.6 224.3,122.1 227.3,122.6 230.4,123.1 233.4,123.6 236.4,124.1 239.4,124.6 242.4,125 245.5,125.4 248.5,125.9 251.5,126.3 254.5,126.7 257.6,127.1 260.6,127.4 263.6,127.8 266.6,128.2 269.6,128.5 272.7,128.8 275.7,129.2 278.7,129.5 281.7,129.8 284.8,130.1 287.8,130.4 290.8,130.7 293.8,130.9 296.8,131.2 299.9,131.5 302.9,131.7 305.9,132 308.9,132.2 312,132.4 315,132.6 318,132.9\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A curve that falls toward the horizontal axis.","steps":["Confirm that the errors tend to zero.","Form the successive ratios of one error over the previous error.","If those ratios tend to a limit strictly between 0 and 1, classify the rate as linear.","If those ratios tend to 0, form the ratios of one error over the square of the previous error.","If the ratios against the square stay bounded, classify the rate as quadratic."]},{"id":"convergence-rates-r2-q2","version":1,"round":2,"type":"multi","prompt":"Which of these positive sequences converge to zero superlinearly?","options":["\\(e_k = (1/2)^k\\)","\\(e_k = (1/2)^{k^2}\\)","\\(e_k = 1/k\\)","\\(e_k = 1/k!\\)","\\(e_k = (9/10)^k\\)"],"explanation":"Superlinear convergence means the successive ratio tends to 0.\n• \\((1/2)^{k^2}\\) has ratio \\((1/2)^{2k+1}\\), which tends to 0.\n• \\(1/k!\\) has ratio \\(1/(k+1)\\), which tends to 0.\n• \\((1/2)^k\\) has ratio \\(1/2\\), a linear rate.\n• \\(1/k\\) has ratio tending to 1, a sublinear rate.\n• \\((9/10)^k\\) has ratio \\(9/10\\), also linear.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a slowly falling curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">k</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">e</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,43.1 52,46.9 55.1,50.5 58.1,53.8 61.1,57 64.1,60 67.2,62.8 70.2,65.5 73.2,68 76.2,70.4 79.2,72.7 82.3,74.9 85.3,77 88.3,79 91.3,80.9 94.4,82.7 97.4,84.4 100.4,86.1 103.4,87.7 106.4,89.2 109.5,90.7 112.5,92.1 115.5,93.5 118.5,94.8 121.6,96 124.6,97.2 127.6,98.4 130.6,99.6 133.6,100.7 136.7,101.7 139.7,102.7 142.7,103.7 145.7,104.7 148.8,105.6 151.8,106.5 154.8,107.4 157.8,108.2 160.8,109.1 163.9,109.8 166.9,110.6 169.9,111.4 172.9,112.1 176,112.8 179,113.5 182,114.2 185,114.8 188,115.5 191.1,116.1 194.1,116.7 197.1,117.3 200.1,117.9 203.2,118.4 206.2,119 209.2,119.5 212.2,120 215.2,120.6 218.3,121.1 221.3,121.5 224.3,122 227.3,122.5 230.4,122.9 233.4,123.4 236.4,123.8 239.4,124.3 242.4,124.7 245.5,125.1 248.5,125.5 251.5,125.9 254.5,126.3 257.6,126.6 260.6,127 263.6,127.4 266.6,127.7 269.6,128.1 272.7,128.4 275.7,128.8 278.7,129.1 281.7,129.4 284.8,129.7 287.8,130 290.8,130.3 293.8,130.6 296.8,130.9 299.9,131.2 302.9,131.5 305.9,131.8 308.9,132.1 312,132.3 315,132.6 318,132.9\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A curve that drops at first and then flattens.","correct_indices":[1,3]},{"id":"convergence-rates-r2-q3","version":1,"round":2,"type":"symbolic","prompt":"The errors obey \\(e_{k+1} = c\\, e_k\\) with \\(0 < c < 1\\), and \\(e_0 = a\\). The error \\(e_3\\) equals","answer":"a*c**3","tolerance":0.001,"explanation":"Apply the recurrence three times: \\(e_1 = c a\\), \\(e_2 = c^2 a\\), and \\(e_3 = c^3 a\\). Both names are required. The product \\(a \\cdot c\\) is only \\(e_1\\). The power \\(c^3\\) drops the starting error. The power \\(a^3 c\\) swaps the roles of the starting error and the rate.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a falling curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">k</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">e</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,41.9 52,44.8 55.1,47.5 58.1,50.2 61.1,52.8 64.1,55.3 67.2,57.7 70.2,60.1 73.2,62.4 76.2,64.6 79.2,66.8 82.3,68.9 85.3,71 88.3,73 91.3,74.9 94.4,76.8 97.4,78.6 100.4,80.4 103.4,82.1 106.4,83.8 109.5,85.4 112.5,87 115.5,88.5 118.5,90 121.6,91.4 124.6,92.8 127.6,94.2 130.6,95.5 133.6,96.8 136.7,98.1 139.7,99.3 142.7,100.4 145.7,101.6 148.8,102.7 151.8,103.8 154.8,104.8 157.8,105.8 160.8,106.8 163.9,107.8 166.9,108.7 169.9,109.6 172.9,110.5 176,111.4 179,112.2 182,113 185,113.8 188,114.5 191.1,115.3 194.1,116 197.1,116.7 200.1,117.4 203.2,118 206.2,118.7 209.2,119.3 212.2,119.9 215.2,120.5 218.3,121 221.3,121.6 224.3,122.1 227.3,122.6 230.4,123.1 233.4,123.6 236.4,124.1 239.4,124.6 242.4,125 245.5,125.4 248.5,125.9 251.5,126.3 254.5,126.7 257.6,127.1 260.6,127.4 263.6,127.8 266.6,128.2 269.6,128.5 272.7,128.8 275.7,129.2 278.7,129.5 281.7,129.8 284.8,130.1 287.8,130.4 290.8,130.7 293.8,130.9 296.8,131.2 299.9,131.5 302.9,131.7 305.9,132 308.9,132.2 312,132.4 315,132.6 318,132.9\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A curve that falls toward the horizontal axis.","variables":[{"name":"a","min":0.1,"max":0.8},{"name":"c","min":0.2,"max":0.8}],"alternates":["a*c*c*c","a*c^3"]},{"id":"convergence-rates-r3-q1","version":1,"round":3,"type":"error","prompt":"A note classifies the errors \\(1\\), \\(1/2\\), \\(1/4\\), \\(1/8\\), and so on. Exactly one step is a false claim.","explanation":"Halving at every step sends the errors to 0, and the ratio is the constant \\(1/2\\), which lies in \\((0, 1)\\). That is linear convergence. It is not quadratic. The quadratic test looks at \\(e_{k+1}/e_k^2 = (1/2)/e_k\\), and that quantity grows without bound as the error shrinks. A bounded quadratic ratio would have to stay finite.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a falling curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">k</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">e</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,41.9 52,44.8 55.1,47.5 58.1,50.2 61.1,52.8 64.1,55.3 67.2,57.7 70.2,60.1 73.2,62.4 76.2,64.6 79.2,66.8 82.3,68.9 85.3,71 88.3,73 91.3,74.9 94.4,76.8 97.4,78.6 100.4,80.4 103.4,82.1 106.4,83.8 109.5,85.4 112.5,87 115.5,88.5 118.5,90 121.6,91.4 124.6,92.8 127.6,94.2 130.6,95.5 133.6,96.8 136.7,98.1 139.7,99.3 142.7,100.4 145.7,101.6 148.8,102.7 151.8,103.8 154.8,104.8 157.8,105.8 160.8,106.8 163.9,107.8 166.9,108.7 169.9,109.6 172.9,110.5 176,111.4 179,112.2 182,113 185,113.8 188,114.5 191.1,115.3 194.1,116 197.1,116.7 200.1,117.4 203.2,118 206.2,118.7 209.2,119.3 212.2,119.9 215.2,120.5 218.3,121 221.3,121.6 224.3,122.1 227.3,122.6 230.4,123.1 233.4,123.6 236.4,124.1 239.4,124.6 242.4,125 245.5,125.4 248.5,125.9 251.5,126.3 254.5,126.7 257.6,127.1 260.6,127.4 263.6,127.8 266.6,128.2 269.6,128.5 272.7,128.8 275.7,129.2 278.7,129.5 281.7,129.8 284.8,130.1 287.8,130.4 290.8,130.7 293.8,130.9 296.8,131.2 299.9,131.5 302.9,131.7 305.9,132 308.9,132.2 312,132.4 315,132.6 318,132.9\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A curve that falls toward the horizontal axis.","steps":["The errors are positive and tend to 0.","Every successive ratio \\(e_{k+1}/e_k\\) equals \\(1/2\\).","A constant ratio strictly between 0 and 1 means the convergence is linear.","That same constant ratio means the convergence is quadratic."],"flaw_index":3,"reasons":["The ratio against the square of the error is unbounded, so the rate is not quadratic.","The successive ratio is not \\(1/2\\).","A constant ratio in \\((0, 1)\\) is superlinear rather than linear."],"correct_reason":0},{"id":"convergence-rates-r3-q2","version":1,"round":3,"type":"numeric","prompt":"The errors begin \\(1/2\\), \\(1/4\\), \\(1/16\\). This is the start of \\(e_{k+1} = e_k^2\\). What is \\(e_2 / e_1^2\\)?","answer":1.0,"unit":"1","tolerance":0.02,"explanation":"Index them as \\(e_0 = 1/2\\), \\(e_1 = 1/4\\), \\(e_2 = 1/16\\). Then \\(e_1^2 = 1/16\\), and the ratio is \\(1\\). The same value is \\(e_1 / e_0^2\\). A quadratic rate allows this ratio to stay bounded. Here it equals 1 at every step. Reporting \\(1/4\\) is the successive ratio \\(e_2/e_1\\), which is the superlinear test rather than the quadratic one. Reporting \\(1/16\\) repeats \\(e_2\\).","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a line sloping down</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">t</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">z</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,40.1 52,41.1 55.1,42.1 58.1,43.2 61.1,44.2 64.1,45.3 67.2,46.3 70.2,47.4 73.2,48.4 76.2,49.4 79.2,50.5 82.3,51.5 85.3,52.6 88.3,53.6 91.3,54.7 94.4,55.7 97.4,56.7 100.4,57.8 103.4,58.8 106.4,59.9 109.5,60.9 112.5,62 115.5,63 118.5,64 121.6,65.1 124.6,66.1 127.6,67.2 130.6,68.2 133.6,69.3 136.7,70.3 139.7,71.3 142.7,72.4 145.7,73.4 148.8,74.5 151.8,75.5 154.8,76.6 157.8,77.6 160.8,78.6 163.9,79.7 166.9,80.7 169.9,81.8 172.9,82.8 176,83.9 179,84.9 182,85.9 185,87 188,88 191.1,89.1 194.1,90.1 197.1,91.2 200.1,92.2 203.2,93.2 206.2,94.3 209.2,95.3 212.2,96.4 215.2,97.4 218.3,98.5 221.3,99.5 224.3,100.5 227.3,101.6 230.4,102.6 233.4,103.7 236.4,104.7 239.4,105.8 242.4,106.8 245.5,107.8 248.5,108.9 251.5,109.9 254.5,111 257.6,112 260.6,113 263.6,114.1 266.6,115.1 269.6,116.2 272.7,117.2 275.7,118.3 278.7,119.3 281.7,120.3 284.8,121.4 287.8,122.4 290.8,123.5 293.8,124.5 296.8,125.6 299.9,126.6 302.9,127.6 305.9,128.7 308.9,129.7 312,130.8 315,131.8 318,132.9\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A straight line sloping downward on labeled axes."},{"id":"convergence-rates-r3-q3","version":1,"round":3,"type":"mc","prompt":"On a plot of \\(\\log e_k\\) against the iteration index, linear convergence looks like","options":["a horizontal line","a straight line with negative slope","a curve that becomes vertical in a single step and then stops","a straight line with positive slope"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"If \\(e_{k+1} = \\mu e_k\\) with \\(0 < \\mu < 1\\), then \\(\\log e_k = \\log e_0 + k \\log \\mu\\). The slope \\(\\log \\mu\\) is negative, so the plot is a descending straight line. A horizontal line would be a constant error. A positive slope would be growth. A single vertical drop is not the picture of a constant factor applied at every iteration.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>a falling curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/>